[Music]  skyline of houston there where a doctor  says he was punished for trying to save  lives and this morning he's ready to  strike back dr assan gokal was fired  from his job and charged with a crime  after giving out covet vaccine doses  that were on the brink of expiration  this was at the end of last year when  vaccine doses were often very scarce and  apartment and appointments were very  hard to find our lead national  correspondent david begneau is in  houston for us where we've just learned  that dr goelkel plans to sue the county  government that fire this has always  been a perplexing case david good  morning  good morning we just confirmed through  sources that within the next 72 hours dr  hassan gokul is expected to file that  lawsuit claiming that he was fired on  the basis of race and national origin  look the headlines were really big and  prominent international when the story  broke doctor accused of stealing covet  19 vaccine people were shocked how could  you do that but the headlines were not  as big and prominent when the entire  criminal case brought by the district  attorney against dr gokul completely  fell  apart breaking news a harris county  public health doctor is facing charges  for stealing a vial of the covid19  vaccine it was on the local news where  dr hassan gokul says he first found out  that harris county district attorney kim  ogg had charged him with theft by a  public servant did the da's office call  you no did you hear from any  investigators no  nobody no here's how this all started it  was december 29 2020 vaccines were just  rolling out nationally and harris county  was having its first public vaccination  event and dr gokul was the supervisor at  the end of the night he said there was  one open vial with 10 doses left in that  vial and they had to give them away  within six hours or they were going to  expire i made a quick call to the  immediate supervisor said hey  i've got  a few extra doses i'm going to find  people to try and give them to and the  answer was okay  i started making phone calls to people  that i thought  would know  somebody or might have  contacts who may  benefit from that vaccine  specifically in the high risk categories  zara ahmed was one of them she works at  a doctor's office and her mother who  lives with her has parkinson's disease  she defends dr gokul how was a stealing  i mean i i don't think it was  there's no way you could call this  stealing  dr gogle says when there was just one  dose left and less than an hour before  it expired  he gave the vaccination to his wife  maria who has pulmonary sarcoidosis  which is an inflammatory disease of the  lungs the next day he says he turned in  all the paperwork for the 10 doses that  he gave out and eight days after that he  said his hr director said he wanted to  see him we heard that you uh  my that you took a vial  and vaccinated your friends and family  with it what am i  are you talking about  the first day of vaccination when there  were leftover vaccines  and i took them and i found 10 people to  give them to  oh you admitted  well i'm gonna have to terminate you  you guys were gonna throw these away and  i found people to give them to  he said well but you know the other part  of it is is that you didn't do it  in a  in an equitable manner  are you suggesting that there were too  many indian names in the group  he points fingers exactly  the investigation report from the  district attorney's office stated that  the hr director did say that the patient  forms that they had identified were  individuals of the same nationality as  dr gokul so at that point it looked like  the forms belonged to friends and family  two weeks after dr gokul was fired the  district attorney put out a press  release claiming that dr gokul abused  his position to place his friends and  family in line in front of people and  that he disregarded county protocols but  five days later assistant district  attorney kimberly smith told dr gokul's  lawyer in an email that there were not  any written protocols at that time  nor was there a wait list harris county  judge franklin bynum reviewed the  district attorney's case against dr  gokul and he found that there was no  probable cause to charge the doctor  based on the  the information they provided me that  not only had they not proven that there  was theft but that they had gone about  it in a way that was pretty incomplete  and pretty sloppy two months later the  texas medical board cleared dr gokul of  any wrongdoing but the d.a persisted and  four months after that she took the case  to a grand jury and the grand jury  declined to indict dr gokul the  spokesperson for the district attorney's  office told us that the grand jury has  spoken and we greatly respect that  adding given grand jury secrecy and the  possibility of civil litigation  involving the county it would be  inappropriate for the d.a to participate  in an interview  paul doyle is dr gokul's attorney well  that's ironic because she finds herself  in front of every camera whenever it's  self-serving to her say sorry okay how  about say i got it wrong  this has been incredibly incredibly  devastating and there's no other way to  put it tell folks why you did what you  did  i did  what a physician would do  which is  take resources meant for patients and  people and give it to them  instead of throwing them away and that's  why it was so infuriating

